The prototype measured in docs/mid-drive-recovery-plan.md, as a patch to
ios/Sources at 191e15c, on top of tools/mid_drive_personas.patch. Apply it to a
scratch copy, never to the tree:

    git archive 191e15c ios tools | tar -x -C <scratch>
    cd <scratch> && git apply <repo>/tools/mid_drive_personas.patch \
                 && git apply <repo>/tools/mid_drive_prototype.patch

It is evidence for the plan, not the implementation: it is written to be
measured, not reviewed. What it adds:
- Geo.swift: `progress(... notAfter:)`, an upper bound, and `passes(...)`,
  every pass of the line near a point with its direction;
- NavigationModel.swift: the wrong-way detector (plan, section 4.2, armed on
  course-aligned forward progress), the loop far-point cap (4.4), failed
  requests counted apart from the reroute backoff with a `networkReachable`
  seam and `connectivityRestored()` (3.1), `connectivity` and `distanceToLine`
  for the banner;
- VoiceGuide.swift: `announceRecovery`, one latched prompt;
- NavView.swift: the banner rows of the plan's section 2 table;
- the harness: deadZone / loopDeadZone (no path, then the path returns), and
  wrong-way detections recorded per drive and per replay;
- ios/Tests/BannerShotTests.swift, which renders the real NavView in each new
  banner state on a fixture route (TEST_RUNNER_SUNDAYDRIVE_SHOTS=<dir>).

